2026-10-16 06:31:33,441 | INFO | src.pipeline | Final Answer:**Company policy on using external AI tools**

| What the policy says | What you should do |
|----------------------|--------------------|
| **Do not** enter any confidential or restricted data into an external AI system. This includes: <ul><li>Customer‑personal or confidential information</li><li>Private employee data (e.g., HR records, salaries)</li><li>Passwords, API keys, or other security credentials</li><li>Proprietary source code or technical designs</li><li>Unreleased product, marketing, financial or strategic information</li></ul> | Keep that data inside approved, company‑controlled systems. If you’re unsure whether something is “restricted,” treat it as confidential and do not copy it into an AI tool. |
| You may only use an external AI tool **if** the organization has **explicitly approved** the tool **and** the specific use‑case. | Check the approved‑tools list (usually maintained by IT/Information Security) or ask your manager/IT before trying a new AI service. |
| Any content that **is** generated by an AI tool must be **reviewed and validated** by an employee before it is used for: <ul><li>Business‑critical decisions</li><li>Customer communications</li><li>Legal, compliance, or financial documents</li><li>Any high‑impact activity</li></ul> | Treat AI output like a draft: proofread, verify accuracy, and ensure it meets quality, confidentiality, and compliance standards before publishing or acting on it. |
| The company may monitor the use of its systems where allowed by law and policy. | Assume that anything you do on a company‑managed device could be logged. Do not rely on “privacy” when using external services. |
| Employees remain responsible for the **accuracy, quality, confidentiality, and appropriateness** of work produced with AI assistance. | Even when an AI tool is approved, you are still accountable for the final result. |

**Bottom line:**  
Only use external AI tools that have been formally approved, and never paste confidential, proprietary, or any other restricted company data into them. When you do use an approved tool, always double‑check the AI‑generated output before it goes out or is used for important decisions. If you have any doubt, reach out to your manager, the IT security team, or HR for clarification.
